1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,491
You merge a refactoring branch. Git reports
a conflict and says that the other side

2
00:00:09,531 --> 00:00:13,734
deleted the metrics module. You open
the file to look for the markers.

3
00:00:13,774 --> 00:00:18,826
There are none. You ask Ravi why he deleted
the module, and he says he didn't delete

4
00:00:18,866 --> 00:00:21,553
anything. He moved it
and rewrote part of it.

5
00:00:21,893 --> 00:00:26,901
Both of you are telling the truth, and so
is Git. A merge that stops without markers

6
00:00:26,941 --> 00:00:31,829
is the one that stops engineers, because
the routine from the last video, open the

7
00:00:31,869 --> 00:00:35,494
file and edit between the markers,
has nothing to hold on to.

8
00:00:35,534 --> 00:00:40,482
This video replaces that routine with a
better one: read the type, read the stages,

9
00:00:40,522 --> 00:00:45,169
then decide. Keep Ravi's answer in mind.
One number on screen will explain it.

10
00:00:47,009 --> 00:00:50,693
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:50,733 --> 00:00:55,637
A merge joins another branch's history
into yours, and a conflict is a path where

12
00:00:55,677 --> 00:01:00,459
Git can't decide the result on its own.
So far every conflict you've seen was a

13
00:01:00,499 --> 00:01:05,586
content conflict: two sides changed the same
lines, and Git wrote marker blocks into

14
00:01:05,626 --> 00:01:09,432
the file. That's one type out of
eight in this chapter's table.

15
00:01:09,772 --> 00:01:14,983
Today you meet the others: add against add,
modify against delete, the three rename

16
00:01:15,023 --> 00:01:19,538
cases, binary files, a file against
a directory, and a directory rename.

17
00:01:19,578 --> 00:01:24,662
For each one you do the same three things.
You read the word in parentheses after

18
00:01:24,702 --> 00:01:28,269
CONFLICT. You read the two
letters in git status --short.

19
00:01:28,309 --> 00:01:33,456
And you read which stages exist in the
index, Git's draft of the next commit, with

20
00:01:33,496 --> 00:01:38,454
git ls-files -u. Those three readings
tell you which command resolves the path.

21
00:01:38,794 --> 00:01:42,704
Everything here is Git, on your
machine. The project is evalkit.

22
00:01:44,544 --> 00:01:46,123
Here's what you'll walk away with.

23
00:01:46,463 --> 00:01:47,875
After this video you can:

24
00:01:48,215 --> 00:01:51,444
Name the conflict type from
the line git status prints.

25
00:01:51,784 --> 00:01:56,293
Resolve a rename against an edit and explain
the role of the similarity threshold.

26
00:01:56,633 --> 00:02:01,915
Resolve a modify/delete conflict in either
direction from the stages that exist.

27
00:02:02,255 --> 00:02:05,594
Resolve a conflict in a binary
file by choosing a version.

28
00:02:05,934 --> 00:02:10,072
Explain a directory-rename conflict and
the setting that controls its detection.

29
00:02:11,912 --> 00:02:17,029
Why are there types at all? Because the
three-way rule works on paths before it

30
00:02:17,069 --> 00:02:22,250
works on lines. For each path Git looks
at three things: what the base had, what

31
00:02:22,290 --> 00:02:27,407
ours has, what theirs has. Here's one
small history from today's demo, with its

32
00:02:27,447 --> 00:02:31,388
branch names. 6ae3c51 is the
base. e5b08ff, on main, is ours.

33
00:02:31,428 --> 00:02:34,260
727adba, on a refactoring
branch, is theirs.

34
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:39,549
A content conflict is the case where all
three have the file and both sides changed

35
00:02:39,589 --> 00:02:44,597
it in overlapping places. But a path may
also be missing on one side, missing in the

36
00:02:44,637 --> 00:02:49,465
base, or present under a different name.
Each of those combinations is a conflict

37
00:02:49,505 --> 00:02:52,651
type, and most of them never
read a line of the file.

38
00:02:52,991 --> 00:02:57,625
That gives you the first fact of this
video, in the textbook's words: the word in

39
00:02:57,665 --> 00:03:02,185
parentheses after CONFLICT names the type,
and only some types put markers in a

40
00:03:02,225 --> 00:03:02,473
file.

41
00:03:02,813 --> 00:03:07,567
The second fact is about the stages,
the numbered slots of an index entry.

42
00:03:07,607 --> 00:03:11,196
Stage 1 is the base, stage 2
is ours, stage 3 is theirs.

43
00:03:11,236 --> 00:03:16,508
In a content conflict all three exist. In
the other types some are absent, and the

44
00:03:16,548 --> 00:03:21,950
absence is the information. No stage 1 means
there was no common version: both sides

45
00:03:21,990 --> 00:03:26,939
added the path. No stage 3 means "their
version" is no file: they deleted it.

46
00:03:26,979 --> 00:03:32,122
One stage alone, on a path you didn't
create, means Git is proposing a location.

47
00:03:32,462 --> 00:03:36,361
The third fact is about renames.
Git does not record renames.

48
00:03:36,401 --> 00:03:41,526
A tree, Git's listing of one directory,
stores names and blob IDs, nothing else.

49
00:03:41,566 --> 00:03:46,757
A blob is the stored content of one file.
The merge detects a rename by comparing

50
00:03:46,797 --> 00:03:51,083
content, with the same 50 percent
similarity threshold as git diff.

51
00:03:51,423 --> 00:03:56,202
When detection succeeds, a rename on
one side and an edit on the other is no

52
00:03:56,242 --> 00:03:59,690
conflict at all: your edit
arrives in the renamed file.

53
00:03:59,730 --> 00:04:04,383
That's the history on screen: e5b08ff
is an edit, and 727adba is a rename.

54
00:04:04,423 --> 00:04:09,202
When detection fails, the same change
looks like a deletion and an unrelated

55
00:04:09,242 --> 00:04:12,310
addition, and you get the
conflict from the hook.

56
00:04:12,650 --> 00:04:17,847
When does detection fail? When a move and
a rewrite land in one commit, so that less

57
00:04:17,887 --> 00:04:21,151
than half of the content
survives under the new name.

58
00:04:21,191 --> 00:04:25,952
A rename is an inference from similarity,
and every inference needs a cutoff.

59
00:04:26,292 --> 00:04:30,820
The fourth fact is about binary files.
Git can't merge them line by line.

60
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:36,075
It reports the conflict as a content conflict,
records all three stages, leaves your

61
00:04:36,115 --> 00:04:39,204
version in the working tree
and writes no markers.

62
00:04:39,544 --> 00:04:44,386
And the resolving commands follow from the
stages. git add means "this path exists

63
00:04:44,426 --> 00:04:49,148
in the result, with this content". git rm
means "this path does not exist in the

64
00:04:49,188 --> 00:04:54,149
result". Every type in today's table is
resolved with some combination of those two,

65
00:04:54,189 --> 00:04:56,114
sometimes after choosing a stage.

66
00:04:57,954 --> 00:05:02,411
A picture helps. Two people reorganize
the same filing cabinet from the same

67
00:05:02,451 --> 00:05:03,535
starting inventory.

68
00:05:03,875 --> 00:05:08,600
A content conflict is both of them rewriting
the same paragraph of one document.

69
00:05:08,640 --> 00:05:13,484
The other types are about the folders. One
person moved a document while the other

70
00:05:13,524 --> 00:05:17,058
shredded it. Both filed a new
document under the same label.

71
00:05:17,098 --> 00:05:21,883
One relabelled a whole drawer while the
other dropped a new document into the old

72
00:05:21,923 --> 00:05:25,635
drawer. Nobody needs to read a
paragraph to see those problems.

73
00:05:25,675 --> 00:05:27,064
They need the inventory.

74
00:05:27,404 --> 00:05:32,423
The stages are that inventory. So the model
is: for a conflict without markers, the

75
00:05:32,463 --> 00:05:33,763
index is the conflict.

76
00:05:34,103 --> 00:05:38,647
Where the picture breaks: a person who
moves a document knows they moved it.

77
00:05:38,687 --> 00:05:43,712
Git does not. It sees a name that vanished
and a name that appeared, and it infers a

78
00:05:43,752 --> 00:05:47,571
move only when the two contents
are at least 50 percent similar.

79
00:05:47,611 --> 00:05:51,732
The filing clerk has a memory. Git
has two snapshots and a threshold.

80
00:05:53,572 --> 00:05:57,922
Here's the whole video in one table,
eight types. Don't memorise it yet.

81
00:05:57,962 --> 00:06:00,177
We fill it in row by row in the demo.

82
00:06:00,517 --> 00:06:03,492
And this box belongs to the
conflict from the hook.

83
00:06:03,532 --> 00:06:05,029
It comes back in the demo.

84
00:06:06,869 --> 00:06:14,418
add/add. labs/run ch08/conflict-add-add.
Both branches created evalkit/cache.py with

85
00:06:14,458 --> 00:06:16,044
different content.

86
00:06:16,384 --> 00:06:20,312
Predict which stages exist.
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

87
00:06:25,152 --> 00:06:30,988
AA, stages 2 and 3, and asking for stage 1
fails: the path is in the index, but not

88
00:06:31,028 --> 00:06:36,653
at stage 1. There was no common version.
This type does write markers, one block

89
00:06:36,693 --> 00:06:38,423
holding both whole files.

90
00:06:38,763 --> 00:06:43,994
Look at the two halves. One is an in-memory
cache, the other loads JSON from disk.

91
00:06:44,034 --> 00:06:48,301
Two modules with the same name are
usually two answers to one need.

92
00:06:48,341 --> 00:06:51,708
The resolution is a design
conversation, not an edit.

93
00:06:52,048 --> 00:06:56,718
modify/delete. labs/run
ch08/conflict-modify-delete.

94
00:06:56,758 --> 00:07:02,968
You edited scripts/legacy_eval.sh,
and the cleanup branch deleted it.

95
00:07:03,308 --> 00:07:08,576
Read the CONFLICT line to the end: "Version
HEAD of scripts/legacy_eval.sh left in

96
00:07:08,616 --> 00:07:12,848
tree." Git keeps the edited version
in the working tree and waits.

97
00:07:12,888 --> 00:07:15,372
The long status says "deleted by them".

98
00:07:15,712 --> 00:07:21,776
UD, stages 1 and 2. Stage 3 does not exist.
Now a question about the shortcuts from

99
00:07:21,816 --> 00:07:27,806
the last video. What do git checkout --theirs
and git restore --theirs 🔴 do with a

100
00:07:27,846 --> 00:07:31,484
path that has no "their
version"? Say it out loud.

101
00:07:36,324 --> 00:07:41,785
They disagree. git checkout --theirs refuses:
the path does not have their version.

102
00:07:41,825 --> 00:07:47,021
git restore --theirs succeeds silently,
exit status 0, and the test -f after it

103
00:07:47,061 --> 00:07:52,456
fails: the file is gone from the working
tree. Restoring "their version" of a path

104
00:07:52,496 --> 00:07:57,759
that they deleted means deleting it.
Neither command marks the path as resolved.

105
00:07:57,799 --> 00:08:03,326
It's still UD. And git restore --merge can't
rebuild a marker file, because the path

106
00:08:03,366 --> 00:08:05,779
does not have all necessary versions.

107
00:08:06,119 --> 00:08:10,474
From the branch that did the deleting, the
same conflict has the letters the other

108
00:08:10,514 --> 00:08:11,010
way round.

109
00:08:11,350 --> 00:08:16,617
DU, stages 1 and 3. Here the decision was
to keep the edited file, and git add 🟢

110
00:08:16,657 --> 00:08:21,924
records that. Back on main, the opposite
decision: the deletion wins, and git rm

111
00:08:21,964 --> 00:08:22,654
records it.

112
00:08:22,994 --> 00:08:27,570
The textbook's sentence for this type:
the question is not "which file wins" but

113
00:08:27,610 --> 00:08:30,801
"why was it deleted, and does
the edit need a new home?"

114
00:08:31,141 --> 00:08:35,701
Renames. labs/run ch08/conflict-rename.
First the good case.

115
00:08:35,741 --> 00:08:42,063
You edited metrics.py on main, and the
refactoring branch renamed it to scoring.py.

116
00:08:42,103 --> 00:08:45,283
Predict: conflict or not? Say it out loud.

117
00:08:50,123 --> 00:08:55,365
No conflict. The summary says rename, 100
percent, and the head at the end shows

118
00:08:55,405 --> 00:09:00,515
your strip() edit inside scoring.py.
Rename detection carried the edit across.

119
00:09:00,855 --> 00:09:03,527
Two different renames of
the same file do conflict.

120
00:09:03,867 --> 00:09:09,385
Three paths, three letter pairs: DD on the
old name, UA on their name, AU on ours,

121
00:09:09,425 --> 00:09:15,078
and one stage per path. All three blobs have
the same ID, because nobody changed the

122
00:09:15,118 --> 00:09:17,789
content. All three paths need an answer.

123
00:09:18,129 --> 00:09:22,963
Try it now. Thirty seconds, on paper. The
decision is that the module is called

124
00:09:23,003 --> 00:09:27,898
scoring. Write the three commands that
answer all three paths, then say them out

125
00:09:27,938 --> 00:09:28,206
loud.

126
00:09:33,046 --> 00:09:36,469
The answer: git add the name
you keep, git rm the other two.

127
00:09:36,509 --> 00:09:41,202
If you wrote only one, you're not alone:
that slip is on today's list of mistakes.

128
00:09:41,542 --> 00:09:44,832
A rename against a deletion is
reported under the new name.

129
00:09:45,172 --> 00:09:50,521
Now the hook. Ravi moved metrics.py to
scoring.py and rewrote most of it in the same

130
00:09:50,561 --> 00:09:50,970
commit.

131
00:09:51,310 --> 00:09:56,288
Predict what the first diff --summary
reports, and what the merge will call the

132
00:09:56,328 --> 00:09:57,939
conflict. Say it out loud.

133
00:10:02,779 --> 00:10:08,083
With the default threshold: a delete and a
create. With a threshold of 30 percent: a

134
00:10:08,123 --> 00:10:12,472
rename at 39 percent. There's the
number that explains Ravi's answer.

135
00:10:12,512 --> 00:10:17,307
Only 39 percent of the content survived,
so the merge sees a deletion and an

136
00:10:17,347 --> 00:10:19,597
addition, and reports modify/delete.

137
00:10:19,937 --> 00:10:23,377
He moved the file, Git saw a
deletion, and both are right.

138
00:10:23,417 --> 00:10:27,758
Look at the last status line: scoring.py
is staged, A, without your edit.

139
00:10:27,798 --> 00:10:32,680
If you ran git rm on the old path now, your
edit would be gone and the merge would

140
00:10:32,720 --> 00:10:33,520
look finished.

141
00:10:33,860 --> 00:10:38,735
The fix is to abort and tell the
merge to use a lower threshold.

142
00:10:38,775 --> 00:10:43,035
git merge -X find-renames=30%
🟡 CAUTION, as every merge.

143
00:10:43,375 --> 00:10:49,245
The same merge is now a content conflict
inside the new file, which is the conflict

144
00:10:49,285 --> 00:10:54,443
you can reason about. Look at the
labels: HEAD:evalkit/metrics.py against

145
00:10:54,483 --> 00:11:00,424
refactor/score-objects:evalkit/scoring.py.
They include paths, because the two sides

146
00:11:00,464 --> 00:11:02,987
know the file under different names.

147
00:11:03,327 --> 00:11:07,071
Binary. labs/run ch08/conflict-binary.

148
00:11:07,411 --> 00:11:13,529
A warning that binary files cannot be merged,
then CONFLICT (content), UU, and three

149
00:11:13,569 --> 00:11:18,000
stages. git diff says only "Binary
files differ". Quick quiz.

150
00:11:18,040 --> 00:11:22,324
Which version is in the working
tree? A, the base. B, ours.

151
00:11:22,364 --> 00:11:24,010
C, theirs. Your answer?

152
00:11:28,850 --> 00:11:34,750
It's B. The hash equals the stage 2 entry,
32a12ec, so it's our version, byte for

153
00:11:34,790 --> 00:11:35,116
byte.

154
00:11:35,456 --> 00:11:40,697
git restore --theirs writes stage 3, the
hash confirms it, and the path stays UU

155
00:11:40,737 --> 00:11:45,979
until git add. For a generated artifact
such as this confusion-matrix image, the

156
00:11:46,019 --> 00:11:50,072
honest resolution is to regenerate
it from the merged sources.

157
00:11:50,412 --> 00:11:55,080
File against directory. labs/run
ch08/conflict-file-directory.

158
00:11:55,120 --> 00:12:00,319
One branch added a file named docs,
the other a directory named docs.

159
00:12:00,659 --> 00:12:06,239
Both cannot exist, so Git moved the file
aside to docs~HEAD, a name that says where

160
00:12:06,279 --> 00:12:07,119
it came from.

161
00:12:07,459 --> 00:12:11,840
Directory rename. labs/run
ch08/conflict-directory-rename.

162
00:12:11,880 --> 00:12:17,709
You moved the package under src/. Asha
added a new file in the old directory.

163
00:12:18,049 --> 00:12:23,141
CONFLICT (file location). Git noticed that
the directory was renamed and suggests

164
00:12:23,181 --> 00:12:28,463
where the new file should perhaps go. No
markers, one stage, and the file is already

165
00:12:28,503 --> 00:12:31,504
at the suggested path.
Accepting is one command.

166
00:12:31,844 --> 00:12:35,944
merge.directoryRenames controls
this. Its default is conflict.

167
00:12:35,984 --> 00:12:38,615
The other two values act without asking.

168
00:12:38,955 --> 00:12:44,472
With true Git moves the file and tells you.
With false the merge is clean and wrong:

169
00:12:44,512 --> 00:12:49,302
evalkit/cache.py sits in a directory
that no longer contains the package.

170
00:12:49,342 --> 00:12:51,287
The default costs one git add.

171
00:12:53,127 --> 00:12:54,775
Five mistakes to watch for.

172
00:12:55,115 --> 00:13:00,024
Looking for markers in a modify/delete
conflict and concluding that nothing is

173
00:13:00,064 --> 00:13:05,228
wrong. Root cause: the type is decided on
paths, not on lines, so the working tree

174
00:13:05,268 --> 00:13:10,177
holds the modified version without markers
and the conflict exists only in the

175
00:13:10,217 --> 00:13:10,558
index.

176
00:13:10,898 --> 00:13:14,925
Resolving a below-threshold rename
with git rm on the old path.

177
00:13:14,965 --> 00:13:20,349
Root cause: rename detection failed under
50% similarity, so the new file was staged

178
00:13:20,389 --> 00:13:23,254
without your edit and
the git rm discards it.

179
00:13:23,594 --> 00:13:28,436
Using git restore --theirs on a path
they deleted and expecting an error.

180
00:13:28,476 --> 00:13:33,652
Root cause: there is no stage 3, and git
restore treats "their version" as "no

181
00:13:33,692 --> 00:13:36,527
file", while git checkout
--theirs refuses.

182
00:13:36,867 --> 00:13:40,398
Answering only one path of
a rename/rename conflict.

183
00:13:40,438 --> 00:13:46,166
Root cause: the conflict spans three paths
with one stage each, and each needs a git

184
00:13:46,206 --> 00:13:47,265
add or a git rm.

185
00:13:47,605 --> 00:13:51,291
Setting merge.directoryRenames=false
to stop the questions.

186
00:13:51,331 --> 00:13:56,343
Root cause: the merge then leaves new
files in the old directory without a word,

187
00:13:56,383 --> 00:13:59,437
which is clean for Git and
wrong for the project.

188
00:14:01,277 --> 00:14:06,642
Now, out of the lab. A model-evaluation
team restructures its Python package:

189
00:14:06,682 --> 00:14:12,538
evalkit/ moves under src/, the metrics module
is renamed, and scores become objects.

190
00:14:12,578 --> 00:14:18,434
The refactoring branch lives for two weeks.
Meanwhile four feature branches edit the

191
00:14:18,474 --> 00:14:22,505
old module and one adds a cache
file to the old directory.

192
00:14:22,845 --> 00:14:27,765
On merge day the team sees, in one afternoon,
a file-location conflict, a rename

193
00:14:27,805 --> 00:14:32,662
that carried edits across cleanly, and
one modify/delete on the module that was

194
00:14:32,702 --> 00:14:37,622
moved and rewritten in a single commit.
The third one costs an hour, because the

195
00:14:37,662 --> 00:14:40,659
first reaction was to ask
who deleted the module.

196
00:14:40,999 --> 00:14:45,848
The textbook's advice for exactly this
situation: refactoring branches are where

197
00:14:45,888 --> 00:14:50,981
these types appear together. Announce large
moves, land them quickly, and merge them

198
00:14:51,021 --> 00:14:56,053
into open feature branches the same day.
And from the root-cause box: land the move

199
00:14:56,093 --> 00:15:00,941
as its own change and merge it into every
open branch before the rewrite starts.

200
00:15:02,781 --> 00:15:09,636
Your turn. Do Lab 6.3, "Rename against
edit", in lab-manual/m06-merge.md.

201
00:15:09,976 --> 00:15:11,456
Predict first, in writing:

202
00:15:11,796 --> 00:15:14,006
Will the merge stop? What decides it?

203
00:15:14,346 --> 00:15:18,465
If it does not stop, in which file
will your edit be after the merge?

204
00:15:18,805 --> 00:15:23,397
What would git diff --summary between the
two branch tips print, and which number in

205
00:15:23,437 --> 00:15:26,485
it would have to change for
the outcome to be different?

206
00:15:26,825 --> 00:15:32,417
Then run the lab by hand. The challenge is
Lab 6.4, "Modify against delete", in the

207
00:15:32,457 --> 00:15:37,709
same file. For that one, write down which
stages you expect before you run git

208
00:15:37,749 --> 00:15:38,523
ls-files -u.

209
00:15:40,363 --> 00:15:46,129
Q124: "State the three-way rule for a
single path as a table of base, ours and

210
00:15:46,169 --> 00:15:49,403
theirs. Which rows never
read file content?"

211
00:15:51,743 --> 00:15:56,831
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
draws the table from memory, with a row for

212
00:15:56,871 --> 00:16:01,834
each combination of unchanged, changed
and absent, and gives the result for each

213
00:16:01,874 --> 00:16:06,712
row. It then separates the rows that are
decided by comparing object IDs or by

214
00:16:06,752 --> 00:16:10,839
presence and absence from the row
that needs a line-by-line merge.

215
00:16:10,879 --> 00:16:15,904
It connects that distinction to today's
video: the conflict types without markers

216
00:16:15,944 --> 00:16:21,032
are the rows that never opened the file.
If you can also say why that makes merges

217
00:16:21,072 --> 00:16:23,221
of large repositories fast, say it.

218
00:16:25,061 --> 00:16:27,988
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

219
00:16:28,328 --> 00:16:33,595
The word in parentheses after CONFLICT
names the type, and only some types write

220
00:16:33,635 --> 00:16:39,167
markers. For a conflict without markers,
the stages in the index are the conflict: a

221
00:16:39,207 --> 00:16:44,541
missing stage 1 means both sides added the
path, and a missing stage 2 or 3 means

222
00:16:44,581 --> 00:16:50,113
one side deleted it. Git infers renames from
content with a 50 percent threshold, so

223
00:16:50,153 --> 00:16:54,956
a move plus a rewrite in one commit
can turn into a modify/delete, and -X

224
00:16:54,996 --> 00:16:59,599
find-renames with a lower value turns
it back into a content conflict.

225
00:16:59,939 --> 00:17:05,006
A binary conflict leaves our version in the
working tree and is resolved by choosing

226
00:17:05,046 --> 00:17:09,991
a stage or regenerating the file.
merge.directoryRenames defaults to conflict, and

227
00:17:10,031 --> 00:17:11,875
that default costs one git add.

228
00:17:13,715 --> 00:17:16,444
Read section 8.11 of Chapter 8.

229
00:17:16,784 --> 00:17:22,625
Copy the table of conflict types into your
own notes: type, status letters, stages

230
00:17:22,665 --> 00:17:26,569
present, markers yes or no,
resolving command. Keep it.

231
00:17:26,609 --> 00:17:27,645
Part 9 uses it.

232
00:17:27,985 --> 00:17:32,907
Today a conflict without markers stopped
being a dead end: you read the type, you

233
00:17:32,947 --> 00:17:37,133
read the stages, and then you decide.
Do Lab 6.3 while this is fresh.

234
00:17:37,173 --> 00:17:42,095
Next time: controlling the result of a
merge. Until then, look at the state first

235
00:17:42,135 --> 00:17:44,606
and type second. See you in the next one.
